WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.120
Nobody expected Us to be a simple slasher
film.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.680
On the surface, it's a movie about evil doppelgängers
and all the horror scenarios you can dream

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.230
up with that kind of premise.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.230
Plus scissors.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.370
Scissors suck.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.100
But now that it's here, it's clear that there
are multiple layers that might get lost in

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.429
a casual viewing.

00:00:17.429 --> 00:00:20.550
So let's to dig into all the small details
it had to offer.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.910
Get ready, because we're un-tethering the
mysteries behind Us.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.920
Spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.159
If you were paying attention, you might have
noticed that the entire plot of Us was spelled

00:00:29.159 --> 00:00:31.550
out in the opening seconds of the film.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:36.140
After that long title shot with the rabbits,
Us opens on a TV playing a commercial for

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.230
the Hands Across America movement.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.750
On either side of the TV are VHS tapes, which
included The Man with Two Brains, The Goonies,

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:44.750
and C.H.U.D.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.080
. It turns out that when you put those all
together, you have the movie Us.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.530
In The Man with Two Brains, Steve Martin stars
as a brain surgeon who telepathically connects

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.710
with a disembodied brain in a jar.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.460
Like in Us, it deals with a mental connection
between two physical forms.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:04.640
"Ladies and gentlemen, I can envision a day
when the brains of brilliant men can be kept

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.450
alive in the bodies of dumb people."

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.710
In The Goonies, of course, the kids' adventure
leads them underground just like Adelaide

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:15.320
when she follows her doppelgänger into the
tunnels below the surface.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:19.359
One of the lines that Red says in the climax
of the film actually comes directly from The

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:20.359
Goonies.

00:01:20.359 --> 00:01:21.909
She says, "It's our time.

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:26.210
It's our time up there," which echoes one
of Mikey's most famous phrases:

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:27.550
"Down here it's our time.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.200
It's our time down here."

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.049
Finally, C.H.U.D., or "Cannibalistic Humanoid
Underground Dwellers", deals with mutants

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.220
living in the sewers under New York City who
begin attacking people on the surface.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:42.400
The parallels there probably don't need much
explanation.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.960
So what was up with all that hand-holding
the Tethereds were into?

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:49.590
In the climax, Red explained a bit of it,
but if you weren't around in the '80s, you

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.759
might not be familiar with the crazy charity
stunt that The Washington Post has lovingly

00:01:53.759 --> 00:01:57.490
dubbed "the most Eighties thing to happen
in the Eighties."

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.979
That charity stunt was Hands Across America,
a 1986 event that tried to raise money for

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:07.109
the homeless by having people stand, hand
in hand, across the entire United States.

00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:11.221
The premise was that we're all the same, and
we're all connected, so why not pay $10 to

00:02:11.221 --> 00:02:13.599
make a country-wide umbilical with all your
peers?

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:14.599
Connect!

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:16.540
"Haven't seen you guys since"

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.540
"Hands across America?"

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.560
“That’s what we were just sayin!”

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.560
“We almost made it.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.489
Couple breaks in the chain, right?”

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:24.220
“Hands across America!”

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.819
Peele has explained in interviews that his
vision for the script was heavily influenced

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:30.790
by that moment, and it definitely shows in
Us.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.650
One of the earliest scenes in the film is
that commercial.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:37.769
After that, the references pop up everywhere
young Adelaide is wearing a Hands Across America

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:42.180
shirt under her Thriller shirt when she's
abducted, the evil Red uses the movement to

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.660
organize her Tethered rebellion, and at the
end, all the Tethered form a similar link

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.230
across Santa Cruz maybe even the whole country,
for all we know.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.629
In the original charity event, it was for
the homeless; in Us, it's for the weird psychic

00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:55.890
underground clones.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.800
But the message is the same: "We won't be
forgotten anymore."

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:04.400
In keeping with the partial '80s setting of
Us, there's one Easter egg that almost definitely

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.120
slipped past anyone who isn't a die-hard fan
of the quintessentially '80s teen vampire

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.019
flick The Lost Boys.

00:03:11.019 --> 00:03:13.880
And let's be honest...that's pretty much everyone,
right?

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.469
“Welcome aboard, Michael!”

00:03:17.469 --> 00:03:21.230
Both movies used the Santa Cruz boardwalk
as a filming location, but while The Lost

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.939
Boys changed the town's name, Us was open
about the film's setting.

00:03:24.939 --> 00:03:30.220
So open, in fact, that during the scenes taking
place in 1986, it alluded to a real-life event

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:32.819
that happened there: the filming of The Lost
Boys.

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:37.140
While young Adelaide is walking with her parents,
her mom tells her dad something along the

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.890
lines of "They're filming a movie over by
the carousel."

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:44.250
Peele confirmed in an interview that the line
indeed referenced The Lost Boys, and most

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.069
likely this scene of Kiefer Sutherland on
a carousel.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:50.409
The only way to hit that nail harder on the
head would've been to have young Adelaide

00:03:50.409 --> 00:03:52.239
walk past the sexy jazz man.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.720
Oh, what could have been...

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.390
You definitely noticed the scissors.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:04:01.329
Those golden implements of stabbiness were
everywhere in Us in hands, in bellies, in

00:04:01.329 --> 00:04:02.999
that one guy's crotch, in everything.

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:06.280
And there's no doubt that scissors are scary
horror weapons.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.000
They're sharp, they're in practically every
home in the world, and they're an integral

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.709
part of the scariest jump scene ever filmed.

00:04:12.709 --> 00:04:16.079
[Scissor ghost comin for ya neck]

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:17.540
That's pretty scary, right?

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.669
Because scissors are scary.

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:23.460
But in the Peele-iverse, as usual, there's
a dual meaning to those golden scissors.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.230
Like the cutout paper people, the shadows
as the family walks across the beach for the

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:32.550
first time, and the house of mirrors, the
scissors in Us are both separate and the same

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:33.550
simultaneously.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:36.750
Peele himself explained it best when talking
about the imagery he wanted to create with

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.030
the scissors, saying,

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:42.600
"...the inherent duality of two equal objects
that are connected, and an object that's used

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.810
to sever or cut, is very important to the
film."

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.360
No, wait maybe Tim Heidecker explained it
best:

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.460
"Every star has a sister star, a little bit
like two eyeballs.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:57.280
If you can imagine, if you could see the other
side of my eyeball, you’d see a 360 degree

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.520
eyeball."

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:02.560
There are a lot of themes running through
Us, one of which is the horror-centric idea

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.680
of creatures from the deep coming up to prey
on surface dwellers.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.560
And the biggest nod to that came in the form
of Jason's Jaws shirt.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.180
There's clearly a lot of Jaws influence on
Us.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:16.990
Aside from the idea of predators lurking beneath
us, the scenes on the beach are also reminiscent

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.640
of Steven Spielberg's classic shark thriller.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:24.199
Even one of the specific sequences when Adelaide
realizes that Jason is missing and suddenly

00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:29.620
sees all the innocent beach fun in a series
of sharp, staccato, sinister moments was pieced

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.979
together a lot like the scene in Jaws in which
Chief Brody is on the lookout for a shark

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:34.960
before the big attack.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.830
In an interview with Rolling Stone, Peele
revealed some of the inspiration behind his

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:44.009
latest film, and it comes from an understandable
place The Twilight Zone, one of TV's oldest

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:45.900
and best benchmarks for horror.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:51.010
In particular, Peele pulled inspiration from
a 1960 episode titled "Mirror Image."

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.130
Want to guess what it's about?

00:05:52.130 --> 00:05:53.850
Yup evil doppelgängers.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:57.979
In the episode, a woman at a bus station on
a rainy night sees another person who looks

00:05:57.979 --> 00:05:59.319
exactly like her.

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:04.160
As time goes on, she gets more and more agitated
about it, until she's carted off by the police...at

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.849
which point it's revealed that she wasn't
crazy after all.

00:06:06.849 --> 00:06:10.600
The other woman was actually a mirror image
from another dimension.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.080
"This counterpart, after the two worlds converge,
comes into our world.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.480
And in order to survive, it has to take over."

00:06:19.509 --> 00:06:23.770
The obvious connection is there in Us by making
the antagonists mirror images of the main

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:28.170
characters, but Peele no doubt gave this an
extra visual when he put Adelaide's first

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:32.539
meeting with her Tethered in a literal house
of mirrors with a conveniently placed sign

00:06:32.539 --> 00:06:35.190
that says "Find yourself" at the front door.

